organic farming will be the focus of the  agriculture and Fisheries Council in  Brussels on Monday March the 16th the  ongoing proposal aims to review the  existing legislation on organic  production and labeling of organic  products the goals are mutter  strengthened the development of organic  production in the EU guarantee fair  competition for farmers and improve  consumer confidence in organic products  ministers are expected to give guidance  on sensitive issues such as the presence  of pesticides as well as other forbidden  substances in organic products they will  also debate the import regime and the  control systems of organic products  dairy will also be high on the  minister's agenda as the milk quota  system that has governed the EU market  for over three decades draws to an end  in March 2015 milk quotas have  conditioned the activity of an entire  generation of dairy farmers most of whom  have never produced in an unlimited  environment as a result in some weeks  the EU milk sector will be more  market-oriented this will bring about  changes for dairy producers in a context  of price volatility and market  fluctuation the Commission will brief  ministers on the dairy sector state of  play as well as on current and upcoming  measures ministers will also hold a  debate on the implementation and  simplification of the Common  Agricultural Policy otherwise known as  cap finally the council will be briefed  on animal welfare of furred animals  following a broadcast by Dutch TV and  the publication of a report by an animal  rights organization the Netherlands  would like to address the issue at  council level to prevent products  involving animal welfare mal practices  from entering the EU market
 

